---
/**
 * HoneypotField — anti-spam decoy (Bible §17 anti-abuse). Shared by every
 * capture form so the field name lives in one place and always matches the
 * server check (`website`, see server/validation/common.ts isHoneypotTripped).
 *
 * The wrapper is `display: none`, which removes the input from the
 * accessibility tree entirely — no screen reader reaches it, no axe rule
 * applies, and it needs no visible label — while bots that parse the HTML
 * still fill it. Carries no `id`, so several forms can coexist on one page
 * without colliding. A filled value is never a validation error: the BFF
 * fake-accepts and stores nothing, so the bot learns nothing. Zero JS.
 */
---

<div class="honeypot" aria-hidden="true">
  <input type="text" name="website" value="" tabindex="-1" autocomplete="off" />
</div>

<style>
  .honeypot {
    display: none;
  }
</style>
